 "voice": [
  {
   "id": "vo01",
   "t": 0.1,
   "dur": 4.123,
   "file": "vo/01.wav",
   "text": "Can you count to a billion? Out loud, every single number."
  },
  {
   "id": "vo02",
   "t": 5.1,
   "dur": 9.123,
   "file": "vo/02.wav",
   "text": "A number a second sounds fair, and even at that pace you'd be counting for about thirty-two years. But the numbers don't stay short."
  },
  {
   "id": "vo03",
   "t": 15.3,
   "dur": 7.747,
   "file": "vo/03.wav",
   "text": "Up in the hundreds of millions, every number is a sentence of its own, and you have to say all of it."
  },
  {
   "id": "vo04",
   "t": 24.3,
   "dur": 13.175,
   "file": "vo/04.wav",
   "text": "We know how fast a person really counts, because a man did it, live on the internet, through most of his waking hours, all the way to a million. It took him most of a summer."
  },
  {
   "id": "vo05",
   "t": 39.9,
   "dur": 10.41,
   "file": "vo/05.wav",
   "text": "Keep going at his pace, sleeping when he slept, and the whole count takes nearly four centuries. That's about five whole lifetimes of nothing but numbers."
  },
  {
   "id": "vo06",
   "t": 53.7,
   "dur": 4.736,
   "file": "vo/06.wav",
   "text": "Never sleep, or talk twice as fast, and you'd still need centuries."
  },
  {
   "id": "vo07",
   "t": 59.7,
   "dur": 6.522,
   "file": "vo/07.wav",
   "text": "So the word is easy to say once, and the number is out of reach one by one."
  },
  {
   "id": "vo08",
   "t": 66.9,
   "dur": 4.512,
   "file": "vo/08.wav",
   "text": "Next envelope: if everyone jumped at once, would the Earth move?"
  }
 ]
}